The Dow Jones Industrial Average on Friday was trading lower for a third straight session, with shares of Apple Inc., exacting the biggest toll on the blue-chip gauge. Apple shares (AAPL) were off 3.5%, or about $6, with the magnitude of the drop sapping about 41 points from the Dow in early action. The Dow most recently was down 69 points, or 0.3%, at 24,595, while the S&P 500 index was off 0.3% at 2,684 and the technology-laden Nasdaq Composite Index was down 0.7% at 7,184. Apple's decline may be influenced by downbeat forecasts (http://www.marketwatch.com/story/apple-stock-falls-after-analysts-express-caution-ahead-of-earnings-2018-04-20) for the iPhone maker amid disappointment over chipmaker Taiwan Semiconductor (https://www.cnbc.com/2018/04/19/analysts-worry-apple-iphone-sales-are-even-worse-than-they-thought.html) Manufacturing (2330.TW), which lowered its revenue guidance range for the second quarter, citing weak demand for cell phones. That has led some Wall Street analysts to speculate that weakness in demand bodes ill for Apple Inc. (AAPL).
-Mark DeCambre;
 1013ET
